5. Why can parallelStream() be slower than stream()?
==============================================================================

Answer:
Parallel processing introduces task splitting, thread scheduling,
coordination, and result-combining overhead.

Input:
[1, 2, 3, 4]

Sequential Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(1,2,3,4)
        .stream()
        .map(n -> n * 2)
        .toList();

System.out.println(result);

Output:
[2, 4, 6, 8]


Parallel Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(1,2,3,4)
        .parallelStream()
        .map(n -> n * 2)
        .toList();

System.out.println(result);

Output:
[2, 4, 6, 8]

Important:
The output is the same, but the parallel version may be slower for
such a small dataset because parallel setup costs can exceed the work.

Interview Point:
Use parallelStream() only after benchmarking a sufficiently large,
CPU-intensive, stateless workload.

Avoid for:

Database calls
REST calls
File I/O
Small collections
Shared mutable state

