6. How does Stream processing affect memory consumption?
==============================================================================

Answer:
Memory usage depends on whether the pipeline streams results,
buffers elements, or collects everything into memory.

Input:
[1, 2, 3, 4]

Code Collecting Results:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(1,2,3,4)
        .stream()
        .map(n -> n * 10)
        .toList();

System.out.println(result);

Output:
[10, 20, 30, 40]

Memory Note:
The result List stores all transformed elements.


Code Without Creating a Result List:

List.of(1,2,3,4)
    .stream()
    .map(n -> n * 10)
    .forEach(System.out::println);

Output:
10
20
30
40

Memory Note:
No final result List is created.

Important:
Stateful operations such as sorted() and distinct() may require
buffering or tracking elements.

Interview Point:
Streams do not automatically guarantee low memory consumption.
Collection, sorting, distinct processing, and stateful operations
can still require significant memory.

