10. Why should peek() not contain business logic?
==============================================================================

Answer:
peek() is primarily an observation operation. Putting critical side
effects inside it makes the pipeline difficult to reason about.

Input:
["java", "spring"]

Good Code:

List<String> result =
    List.of("java","spring")
        .stream()
        .peek(System.out::println)
        .map(String::toUpperCase)
        .toList();

System.out.println(result);

Output:
java
spring
[JAVA, SPRING]


Bad Code:

List<String> saved = new ArrayList<>();

List.of("java","spring")
    .stream()
    .peek(saved::add)
    .count();

Problem:
Business behavior becomes dependent on a side effect inside the
Stream pipeline.

Interview Point:
Use peek() for debugging or observation. Use map(), collect(), or
an explicit processing stage for required business operations.

Memory Trick:
peek = See, not Change

