11. When should you create a Custom Collector?
==============================================================================

Answer:
Create a Custom Collector when built-in collectors cannot express
a reusable domain-specific aggregation clearly.

Input:
["Java", "Spring", "Kafka"]

Simple Custom Collector Example:

String result =
    Stream.of("Java","Spring","Kafka")
          .collect(
              StringBuilder::new,
              (sb, value) -> {
                  if(sb.length() > 0) {
                      sb.append(" | ");
                  }
                  sb.append(value);
              },
              (left, right) -> {
                  if(left.length() > 0 && right.length() > 0) {
                      left.append(" | ");
                  }
                  left.append(right);
              })
          .toString();

System.out.println(result);

Output:
Java | Spring | Kafka

Collector Parts:

Supplier
--------
Creates the result container.

Accumulator
-----------
Adds one element to the container.

Combiner
--------
Combines partial containers during parallel execution.

Interview Point:
Use a Custom Collector for reusable domain aggregation, but prefer
built-in collectors when they already express the requirement clearly.

