13. What are the common Parallel Stream pitfalls?
==============================================================================

Answer:
The biggest pitfall is modifying shared mutable state from multiple
worker threads.

Input:
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

Unsafe Code:

List<Integer> result =
    new ArrayList<>();

List.of(1,2,3,4,5)
    .parallelStream()
    .forEach(result::add);

Possible Problem:
Missing values
Unexpected ordering
Data corruption
Race conditions


Safe Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(1,2,3,4,5)
        .parallelStream()
        .map(n -> n * 10)
        .toList();

System.out.println(result);

Output:
[10, 20, 30, 40, 50]

Other Pitfalls:

Small datasets
Blocking operations
Ordering requirements
Non-associative reduction
Shared common ForkJoinPool
Excessive task splitting

Interview Point:
Keep parallel Stream operations stateless, non-interfering, and free
from shared mutation.

