14. Why is Immutable Data Processing important?
==============================================================================

Answer:
Instead of modifying existing values, the pipeline creates a new
result.

Input:
["java", "spring"]

Code:

List<String> source =
    List.of("java","spring");

List<String> result =
    source.stream()
          .map(String::toUpperCase)
          .toList();

System.out.println("Source: " + source);
System.out.println("Result: " + result);

Output:
Source: [java, spring]
Result: [JAVA, SPRING]

Explanation:
The original source remains unchanged.

Important:
Stream.toList() returns an unmodifiable List.

Code:

result.add("KAFKA");

Output:
UnsupportedOperationException

Interview Point:
Immutable processing improves predictability and reduces the risks
associated with shared mutable state and parallel execution.

